in this video you will learn everything  about the leica scale  let's start with the most important  question why do we need a leica scale  let's assume you formulated a hypothesis  and there is a variable that you cannot  measure directly a so-called latent  variable  latent variables are for example  physical well-being or environmental  awareness  why are these variables not directly  measurable  of course you could simply ask in your  questionnaire  what is your level of environmental  awareness the problem is that by just  asking this one question  measuring environmental awareness is  very difficult or very inaccurate  the reason is that environmental  awareness consists of several dimensions  the first person might think about the  question well i'm a member of an animal  protection association and i take care  of distressed animals and maybe that  person therefore ticks very high but on  the other hand he or she makes a long  distance trip by plane three times a  year the next person thinks well my  environmental awareness is very high i  know that we should actually separate  the garbage we shouldn't drive the car  all the time so yes my environmental  awareness is very high but i don't  really care about it so does this person  then take high or low  so of course you can just ask this  single question to measure environmental  awareness but the answers would be quite  useless  now in order to still be able to  adequately measure latent variables like  environmental awareness  you use scales a scale is a group of  questions for example question 1 2 3 and  4  with answer categories from for example  true to not true these questions  together serve to measure a latent  variable by using multiple questions  your measurement becomes more accurate a  scale like this is also called a  multi-item scale the best known scale is  the leicad scale the leicad scale is a  method of measuring personal attitudes  this scale consists of several questions  so-called items  items are statements that record the  degree of agreement or disagreement of  the respondents respondents can thus  indicate how much they disagree or agree  with a statement on the response scale  typically a 4 or 5 level rating is used  the question if a middle category makes  sense or not cannot be answered in  general in some cases it makes sense for  the respondents to have a neutral middle  category available  in order to not be pushed in one  direction on the other hand there is a  risk that many respondents will be happy  to simply choose the middle category  which could make your results less  meaningful now the next question is how  do i come up with the right questions do  i just make the questions myself  that's not a good idea because in that  case you would first have to go through  the effort of checking whether your  questions really measure the latent  variables correctly the right way is to  do a literature and scale research  and find out if there are already  validated questions that you can use to  measure your variables what is the  easiest way to start a literature and  scale research we just start quick and  dirty and google our topic  the next question is how do you analyze  a leica scale the participants in your  survey have now ticked something from  applies to does not apply for questions  1 to 4.  now you can export the finished survey  to excel or data tab then in each row  there is a respondent and in the columns  there are the four questions the first  person check two for question one one  for question two three for question  three and two for question four now you  want to measure your latent variable  with these four questions for leica  scales matrix scale level is often  assumed in practice strictly speaking  the answers on a leicard scale are  ordinarily scaled because the distances  between the answers are not likely to be  perceived as equal by the survey  participants therefore the most common  way is that you build a so-called mean  index for this you simply calculate the  mean value of each row now you have an  estimate for your latent variable you  can also easily calculate the mean index  with data tab to do this copy your data  into this table and click on data  transformation  now you can click here and select  questions 1 to 4 then click on the mean  index and enter the name of the new  variable done  now you can check if your questions  really measure a latent variable you can  do this with the chromebox alpha if you  want to know more about it please have a  look at my video about chromebox alpha  thanks for watching
 

